# .env.example — template for .env.<chain>. use-chain.sh creates .env.<chain>
# from it when missing and fills in what the owner key implies (deploy/README.md,
# "Testing on a testnet"); a remote machine's file is filled in by hand instead.
# An existing .env.<chain> is never overwritten: it is the only place the
# deployment's keys live. Every script and tool reads .env; the simulation's own
# file is .env.local.
#
# The values below are Coston's. For Coston2 change CHAIN_URL, CHAIN_ID,
# ADDRESSES_FILE and NORMAL_PROXY_URL as noted next to each.

# ============================================================================
# Keys — who does what. Each is named for its role, and a test deployment can
# run every role on ONE key: the extension owner's.
# ============================================================================
#
# EXTENSION_OWNER_KEY      The extension owner, and the key every tool signs with
#                          unless it names another role's. Funded on the target chain
#                          (faucet: https://faucet.flare.network/coston). It
#                            - owns the extension: for a reserved id, Flare governance
#                              must have run registerReserved(EXTENSION_ID, <its address>);
#                              or it is on the registry's extension-owner allowlist and
#                              deploy-oracle.sh registers a public id for it;
#                            - wires the extension, allows the image's code hash and
#                              publishes the TEE governance set (wire-extension,
#                              allow-tee-version, set-governance);
#                            - deploys the oracle contracts on a test deployment and
#                              stays their governance, so it publishes configurations
#                              directly (deploy-oracle.sh, publish-local.sh);
#                            - pays the keeper's feed requests (oracle-cycle.sh).
# INITIAL_OWNER            The machines' initial owner, which registration binds them
#                          to and which the default TEE governance set is made of. The
#                          extension owner's address on a test deployment; elsewhere
#                          the machine owner's, whose key is MACHINE_OWNER_KEY.
# PROXY_PRIVATE_KEY        The proxy's signing key: it signs the machine's TEE info
#                          and results, and its address is recorded with the machine.
#                          A key of its own, not the deployer's; it sends no
#                          transactions, so it needs no funds.
# GOVERNANCE_SIGNERS/      The TEE governance set the machine signs with; post-build
# GOVERNANCE_THRESHOLD     registers the same set on chain (set-governance). Default:
#                          INITIAL_OWNER alone, threshold 1.
# MACHINE_OWNER_KEY        Only if the machines are owned by a key other than the
#                          extension owner's: the key of the address in INITIAL_OWNER,
#                          which the registry requires as the sender of a machine's
#                          registration and of its move to production, and as the
#                          caller that pauses it. register-tee and retire-tee sign
#                          with it. That address must also be an allowed machine
#                          owner of the extension, which its owner adds.
# Admin keys               Credentials are installed by admin signatures, not by the
#                          owner: the backing set (1-of-N) authorises the Hex
#                          credentials, the providers set (at least 2 signatures) the
#                          RPC credentials. Their ADDRESSES go in the admins JSON
#                          published with SET_ADMINS (deploy/config/admins/coston.example.json);
#                          their private keys are given to the upload scripts as
#                          ADMIN_KEY / ADMIN_KEYS. For a test the extension owner
#                          can be the backing admin and, with one more key, one of the
#                          two provider admins.

EXTENSION_OWNER_KEY=""
INITIAL_OWNER=""
PROXY_PRIVATE_KEY=""
# GOVERNANCE_SIGNERS="0xAbc...,0xDef..."
# GOVERNANCE_THRESHOLD=2
# MACHINE_OWNER_KEY=""

# ============================================================================
# Chain
# ============================================================================
# The chain this file targets. The scripts take every per-chain default (RPC,
# chain id, normal proxy, faucet) from its row in deploy/scripts/lib/chains.sh;
# the three values below only override that row. (Not named CHAIN: Foundry's
# cast reads that variable as its --chain option.)
#
# Coston has two rows, which share the chain and its indexer and differ in the
# contracts they address: "coston" is the official USDX deployment under the
# reserved extension 1, and "coston_local" a test deployment of our own, made by
# deploy-oracle.sh. Each has its own .env, addresses file and admin set.
FLARE_CHAIN=coston
# CHAIN_URL=                   # the chain's RPC, when not the public one
# CHAIN_ID=                    # 16 coston, 114 coston2, 19 songbird, 14 flare
# ADDRESSES_FILE=              # default: ./deploy/config/$CHAIN/deployed-addresses.json
# The extension id. 1 is the reserved id the official deployment uses, so it is
# the value for coston; on coston_local the registry mints a public id and
# deploy-oracle.sh records it here.
EXTENSION_ID=1
# Where the sibling flare-smart-contracts-v2 checkout is, relative to this repository.
UPSTREAM_DIR=../../../fsp/flare-smart-contracts-v2

# ============================================================================
# Machine
# ============================================================================
# Must be false on live networks.
LOCAL_MODE=false
# true: the machine attests with the test code hash and TEST_PLATFORM (no TEE
# hardware); the registry on Coston and Coston2 accepts that platform. false on
# a real Confidential Space machine — and on Songbird and Flare always false,
# their registries accept only real attestation (set MODE=0 too).
SIMULATED_TEE=true
# Flare's normal (FTDC) proxy on the chain: the availability proof is fetched
# there. Default: the chain's row in deploy/scripts/lib/chains.sh.
# NORMAL_PROXY_URL=
# The machine's public URL: what is registered on chain, what the data providers
# and the keeper reach it on. With the stack on a laptop this is the tunnel in
# front of the proxy's external port (ngrok http 6674); use a static domain, the
# URL is written on chain at registration.
# With the machine deployed remotely (a Confidential Space VM), this is that
# machine's public proxy URL and no tunnel is involved.
# e.g. EXT_PROXY_URL="https://your-static-subdomain.ngrok-free.app" — quote a value
# with unusual characters: the scripts source this file as shell, so an unquoted
# <…> reads as a redirection and every script stops.
EXT_PROXY_URL=
# Only the external port belongs on the tunnel. Keep the internal one on loopback.
EXT_PROXY_INTERNAL_BIND=127.0.0.1:6673
# EXT_PROXY_EXTERNAL_BIND=0.0.0.0:6674

# API key guarding the proxy's POST /direct endpoint. Required on every live
# chain, whose proxy configuration (deploy/config/proxy/extension_proxy.live.toml)
# sets api_key_variable = "DIRECT_API_KEY" and refuses to start without it. The
# scripts hand it to the tools in their environment, never on a command line.
# Two things go through /direct and the key stands
# in front of both: TEE_ORACLE/SET_SECRET (deposits a credential into the
# enclave) and TEE_ORACLE/GET_READINESS (asks the machine to describe itself). A
# price is not one of them, so a leaked key costs availability and provisioning
# safety, not price integrity; treat it as a secret regardless.
# Generate one with: openssl rand -hex 32
DIRECT_API_KEY=""

# ============================================================================
# Provisioning credentials (upload-hex-key.sh, upload-rpc-keys.sh)
# ============================================================================
# DO NOT PUT REAL VALUES HERE unless this file stays on the operator's machine.
# The scripts take them from the environment or a hidden prompt; a value here is
# read by every tool and survives the upload by months.
#
# Which admin set authorises which credential is pinned in the build; how many
# signatures each needs is the published threshold, at or above the floor:
#   hex_api_key, hex_cash_url   backing set,   >= 1 signature   → upload-hex-key.sh
#   every other ref             providers set, >= 2 signatures  → upload-rpc-keys.sh
#
# EXPECTED_CODE_HASH=      # the build you expect, obtained OUT OF BAND (verify-tee);
#                          # with SIMULATED_TEE=true the upload scripts assume tee-node's
#                          # test constant when this is unset
# DIRECT_API_KEY=        # same value as DIRECT_API_KEY above
# ADMIN_KEY=            # one admin private key, hex
# ADMIN_KEYS=           # comma-separated, when one operator holds several; both upload
#                       # scripts sign with every key listed
# BACKING_THRESHOLD=    # upload-hex-key.sh: signatures to collect (default: backing.threshold
#                       # in deploy/config/admins/<chain>.json, else 1)
# PROVIDER_THRESHOLD=   # upload-rpc-keys.sh: signatures to collect (default 2)
#
# The Hex backing credentials. Give them to the chain switch once
# (HEX_API_KEY=… HEX_CASH_URL=… ./deploy/scripts/use-chain.sh <chain>) and the
# scripts do the rest: use-chain.sh writes them here and carries them across later
# switches, publish-local.sh endpoints commits the hex_cash group to HEX_CASH_URL,
# and upload-hex-key.sh hands the URL and the key to the machine. The salt binds
# the published commitment to the provisioned URL and must not change between
# the two; publish-local.sh generates and records it here when missing.
# HEX_API_KEY=
# HEX_CASH_URL=            # https://…, the restricted cash endpoint
# HEX_CASH_SALT=           # openssl rand -hex 32
#
# One per RPC credential, named after its ref in the endpoints configuration:
# ANKR_FLARE_KEY=
# ALCHEMY_FLARE_KEY=

# ============================================================================
# Docker Compose overrides (all have defaults)
# ============================================================================
# SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=            # Unix timestamp for reproducible builds (auto-derived from git by start-services.sh)
# LOG_LEVEL=INFO                # TEE node log level
# MODE=1                        # Attestation backend: 1 simulated (the compose default), 0 production (the image default)
# REDIS_BIND=127.0.0.1:6389    # Host binding for the extension Redis; unset → a free port per run
# COMPOSE_NETWORK=              # Docker network name. The simulation: the external network the base
#                               # compose file joins (default docker_default, the e2e stack's). A live
#                               # chain: the network the stack shares with the chain's indexer (default
#                               # fce-usdx-oracle-<indexer chain>; coston_local uses fce-usdx-oracle-coston)
# INDEXER_DB_BIND=127.0.0.1:3316 # Host binding of the chain indexer's database (debugging only; coston 3316, coston2 3317)
#
# An indexer of your own instead of the bundled docker-compose.<chain>-indexer.yaml
# stack (the proxy configuration is rendered with these):
# INDEXER_DB_HOST=              # empty → the bundled one
# INDEXER_DB_PORT=3306
# INDEXER_DB_NAME=db
# INDEXER_DB_USER=root
# INDEXER_DB_PASSWORD=root
# USE_LOCAL_SIBLINGS=0          # Set to 1 to build node/proxy from on-disk sibling checkouts
# WAIT_TIMEOUT=                 # Timeout for post-build registration waits
# TEE_VERSION=                  # TEE version label for allow-tee-version
